WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.017
This lesson matters because it connects a clean equation to a real safety idea.

2
00:00:05.017 --> 00:00:10.034
When something stops, its momentum changes.

3
00:00:10.034 --> 00:00:15.051
That momentum change can happen in a short harsh time or a longer gentler time.

4
00:00:15.051 --> 00:00:20.068
The total change in momentum might be the same in both cases, but the average force can be very different.

5
00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:25.085
That is the heart of the lesson and the reason impulse matters.

6
00:00:25.085 --> 00:00:30.102
Keep this takeaway in view: Impulse explains why extra stopping time can make the same stop less severe.

7
00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:35.119
Impulse means momentum change.

8
00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:40.136
Impulse is the quantity that links force, time, and change in momentum.

9
00:00:40.136 --> 00:00:45.153
Short stop means large force.

10
00:00:45.153 --> 00:00:50.170
The same change over less time needs a larger average force.

11
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:58.137
On a force-time graph, the area under the graph represents impulse.

12
00:00:58.137 --> 00:01:06.103
That means two very different-looking rectangles or shapes can still represent the same momentum change if their areas are equal.

13
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:14.070
This is a powerful visual because it shows why a lower force acting for longer can have the same overall effect on momentum as a higher force acting for a shorter time.

14
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:22.037
Keep this takeaway in view: Think of impulse as a shape area and a momentum change at the same time.

15
00:01:22.037 --> 00:01:30.003
Area: The area under the force-time graph is the impulse.

16
00:01:30.003 --> 00:01:37.970
Equal area: Equal areas mean the same change in momentum.

17
00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.987
Imagine jumping onto a hard floor and then onto a thick landing mat.

18
00:01:42.987 --> 00:01:48.004
In both cases your momentum has to drop to zero.

19
00:01:48.004 --> 00:01:53.021
The change in momentum is similar, but the mat increases the stopping time.

20
00:01:53.021 --> 00:01:58.038
Because the same change happens over longer time, the average force is smaller.

21
00:01:58.038 --> 00:02:03.055
This analogy is excellent because it turns the abstract formula into a physical design idea.

22
00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:08.072
Keep this takeaway in view: The aim is often not to remove the stop, but to stretch the stop over longer time.

23
00:02:08.072 --> 00:02:13.089
Hard floor means short time.

24
00:02:13.089 --> 00:02:18.106
A short stopping time means a large average force.

25
00:02:18.106 --> 00:02:23.123
Landing mat means longer time.

26
00:02:23.123 --> 00:02:28.140
A longer stopping time reduces the average force.

27
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.968
Impulse equals force times time, and it also equals change in momentum.

28
00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:37.796
So force times time equals change in momentum.

29
00:02:37.796 --> 00:02:42.625
That single bridge is the whole lesson.

30
00:02:42.625 --> 00:02:47.453
It explains the graph, the safety interpretation, and the average-force calculation.

31
00:02:47.453 --> 00:02:52.281
Once the learner sees those as the same story, the topic becomes much easier to reason with.

32
00:02:52.281 --> 00:02:57.109
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the relation as a bridge, not as two disconnected formulas.

33
00:02:57.109 --> 00:03:01.937
Force-time view: Impulse is the area idea and the average-force-times-time idea.

34
00:03:01.937 --> 00:03:06.765
Momentum view: Impulse is the same as the change in momentum.

35
00:03:06.765 --> 00:03:11.594
impulse = F Delta t.

36
00:03:11.594 --> 00:03:16.422
impulse = Delta p.

37
00:03:16.422 --> 00:03:21.250
For average force questions, divide the momentum change by the stopping time.

38
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:26.774
If the momentum change stays fixed and the stopping time doubles, the average force halves.

39
00:03:26.774 --> 00:03:32.298
If the stopping time triples, the average force becomes one third.

40
00:03:32.298 --> 00:03:37.822
This inverse relationship is exactly why crumple zones, airbags, helmets, landing mats, and softer catches matter.

41
00:03:37.822 --> 00:03:43.346
They are impulse-management designs.

42
00:03:43.346 --> 00:03:48.870
They aim to keep the needed momentum change but reduce the average force by making the change take longer.

43
00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:54.394
Keep this takeaway in view: The safety meaning comes from changing time while holding momentum change fixed.

44
00:03:54.394 --> 00:03:59.918
Same Delta p means fixed impulse.

45
00:03:59.918 --> 00:04:05.442
The total required change in momentum does not disappear.

46
00:04:05.442 --> 00:04:10.966
More time means less average force.

47
00:04:10.966 --> 00:04:16.490
Spreading the impulse lowers the force needed each second.

48
00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.811
Try three checks.

49
00:04:19.811 --> 00:04:23.132
What does impulse equal?

50
00:04:23.132 --> 00:04:26.452
Change in momentum.

51
00:04:26.452 --> 00:04:29.773
On a force-time graph, what represents impulse?

52
00:04:29.773 --> 00:04:33.094
The area under the graph.

53
00:04:33.094 --> 00:04:36.415
And if the same momentum change happens over longer time, what happens to the average force?

54
00:04:36.415 --> 00:04:39.735
It decreases.

55
00:04:39.735 --> 00:04:43.056
Those checks are enough to support the whole worked example.

56
00:04:43.056 --> 00:04:46.377
Keep this takeaway in view: The key safety reasoning is now in place before the arithmetic starts.

57
00:04:46.377 --> 00:04:49.698
Impulse meaning means Delta p.

58
00:04:49.698 --> 00:04:53.018
Impulse is the change in momentum.

59
00:04:53.018 --> 00:04:56.339
Graph meaning means area.

60
00:04:56.339 --> 00:04:59.660
Area under the force-time graph gives the impulse.

61
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:05.069
You are ready for the worked example now.

62
00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:10.479
A cyclist's momentum changes by 240 kilogram metres per second in 0.8 seconds while braking.

63
00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:15.888
Start by naming that change in momentum as the impulse.

64
00:05:15.888 --> 00:05:21.297
Then find the average force by dividing 240 by 0.8, which gives 300 newtons.

65
00:05:21.297 --> 00:05:26.706
If the stopping time doubles to 1.6 seconds, the momentum change is still 240 kilogram metres per second.

66
00:05:26.706 --> 00:05:32.116
So divide by the new time instead.

67
00:05:32.116 --> 00:05:37.525
That gives 150 newtons.

68
00:05:37.525 --> 00:05:42.934
The explanation matters as much as the number: the same impulse spread over longer time gives a smaller average force.

69
00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:48.344
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: keep the impulse fixed and compare the forces through time.

70
00:05:48.344 --> 00:05:53.753
Step 1: Recognize the given momentum change as the impulse.

71
00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:59.162
Step 2: Find the average force using the original stopping time.

72
00:05:59.162 --> 00:06:04.571
Delta p = 240 kg m/s.

73
00:06:04.571 --> 00:06:09.981
F = 240 / 0.8 = 300 N.

74
00:06:09.981 --> 00:06:15.390
Keep the impulse fixed while comparing the stopping times.
